---
title: "Global Rate Limiter (Simplified)"
category: "Foundational Infrastructure"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["rate-limiting", "distributed-systems", "edge", "token-bucket", "multi-region"]
---

## Overview

A **global rate limiter** enforces per-principal quotas (API key / user ID / IP / CIDR) across regions without putting cross-region calls on every request.

This simplified design keeps the request path fast by:
- deciding at the edge from a tiny **L1 in-memory cache** when possible
- falling back to a **single regional Rate Limiter Service (RLS)** when needed
- enforcing **global correctness via per-key ownership** (a single “owner region” is the source of truth for each key), with **batched token grants** to other regions

No global coordinator, no global lease database, no Kafka.

---

## Requirements (Kept, Trimmed to Essentials)

### Functional
- Limit by principal: `API_KEY`, `USER_ID`, `IP`, `CIDR` (configurable precedence).
- Throughput limits: **token bucket** (requests/sec or requests/min) with burst.
- Optional concurrency limits: **regional only** (unless explicitly required).
- Multi-dimensional rules via descriptors (endpoint/method/tier/product).
- Deterministic result: `ALLOW|DENY`, `retry_after_ms`, standard headers.
- Admin: CRUD policies, overrides, emergency blocks, `SHADOW` → `ENFORCE`, rollback.
- Observability: allow/deny counts, top offenders, basic debug hooks.

### Non-Functional (Pragmatic)
- Edge latency: L1 hit is sub-ms; RLS call is the “slow path”.
- No cross-region call on every request; cross-region only when a region needs more tokens for a key.
- Policy publication is strongly consistent (versioned snapshots).
- Global quotas are bounded-consistent (bounded by outstanding regional grants).

---

## Simplified Architecture

### High-Level Diagram

```mermaid
graph TB
  C[Clients] --> GW[API Gateway / Edge Proxy]
  GW --> EF[Edge Rate Limit Filter]
  EF --> L1[(L1 In-Memory Cache)]

  EF -->|L1 miss| RLS[Regional Rate Limiter Service]
  RLS --> RRedis[(Regional Redis: local credits + regional-only limits)]

  RLS -->|only when credits low| ORLS[Owner-Region RLS for key]
  ORLS --> ORedis[(Owner Redis: source-of-truth buckets)]

  RLS --> MET[Metrics/Tracing]
  EF --> MET

  RLS --> PSnap[Policy Snapshots (Object Storage/CDN)]
  EF --> PSnap
  RLS --> PAPI[Policy/Admin API]
  PAPI --> PDB[(Postgres Policy DB)]
  PAPI --> PSnap
```

### What “Owner Region” Means (Global Correctness Without a Global Coordinator)

For each `(policy_id, principal, descriptor_hash)`:
- compute `owner_region = consistent_hash(key) % regions`
- the **owner region’s Redis** holds the authoritative bucket
- non-owner regions keep a short-lived **local credit bucket** (tokens granted by the owner)

This yields a single-writer model per key (simple, explainable) while keeping most traffic local.

---

## Request Path (Critical)

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant Client
  participant Edge as Edge Filter + L1
  participant RLS as Regional RLS
  participant RRedis as Regional Redis (local credits)
  participant ORLS as Owner RLS
  participant ORedis as Owner Redis (authoritative)

  Client->>Edge: HTTP request (principal + descriptors)
  Edge->>Edge: L1 check (best-effort cache)
  alt L1 has decision/credits
    Edge-->>Client: ALLOW/DENY + headers
  else L1 miss
    Edge->>RLS: Check(principal, descriptors, policy_version)
    RLS->>RRedis: Consume from local credits (atomic)
    alt credits sufficient
      RLS-->>Edge: decision
    else credits low/empty
      RLS->>ORLS: Reserve(key, desired_tokens)
      ORLS->>ORedis: Atomic consume from authoritative bucket
      ORedis-->>ORLS: granted_tokens + retry_after_ms (if denied)
      ORLS-->>RLS: grant or deny
      RLS->>RRedis: Add grant to local credits (atomic)
      RLS->>RRedis: Consume 1 (atomic)
      RLS-->>Edge: decision
    end
    Edge-->>Client: ALLOW or DENY + headers
  end
```

---

## Policy Distribution (Simplified)

- Policies live in **Postgres**.
- Publishing creates an immutable **snapshot** stored in **object storage** (served via CDN if available).
- Edge filter + RLS **poll** for the latest snapshot (e.g., every 30–60s) and keep last-known-good.
- Optional “push” can be added later, but polling alone is resilient and operationally cheap.

Major simplification: removed pub/sub/Kafka fanout and subscriber services.

---

## Components (Simplified)

### Edge Rate Limiter Filter (Library/Module)
**Responsibilities**
- Policy match from in-memory snapshot.
- Fast-path decision from L1 (tiny TTL cache + single-flight on misses).
- Call regional RLS on L1 miss.
- Emit metrics; avoid heavy per-request logs.

**Kept**
- Single-flight per key on misses.
- Small L1 TTL (seconds) and size cap.

**Removed**
- Any direct dependence on global coordination logic.

---

### Regional Rate Limiter Service (RLS) — Single Service
**Responsibilities**
- Decision API (`Check`).
- Atomic operations in Redis (local credits + regional-only limits).
- Owner routing:
  - if this region is the owner, serve `Reserve` from authoritative buckets
  - if not, call owner region to top up local credits
- Idempotency (optional but small): short TTL per `request_id`.

This is the only “runtime service” besides the gateway filter.

---

### Redis (One Technology, Two Roles)
- **Regional Redis**: local credit buckets + regional-only limits + dedupe keys.
- **Owner Redis**: authoritative buckets for keys owned by this region.
In practice this can be the same Redis cluster per region; the “owner” role is logical (by key ownership), not separate infra.

---

### Policy/Admin API (Can Be the Same Codebase)
- CRUD policies, overrides, emergency blocks.
- Publish snapshot to object storage.
- Rollout modes: `SHADOW` → `ENFORCE`, rollback by switching “current snapshot pointer”.

---

## Algorithms & Semantics (Simplified)

### Token Bucket (Only)
- Parameters: `rate`, `burst`, optional `cost`.
- Stored state: `tokens`, `last_refill_ms`.
- `retry_after_ms` from deficit: `ceil((cost - tokens) / rate)`.

Removed: dual algorithm support (GCRA) unless a clear need exists.

### Global Enforcement Mechanism
- Authoritative consume happens only in the **owner region**.
- Other regions spend from **owner-granted local credits** with short TTL.
- Overshoot is bounded by: `sum(outstanding_local_credits_for_key) + in_flight`.

### Concurrency Limits
- Regional-only semaphore per key (increment on start, decrement on completion).
- Make “global concurrency” an explicit opt-in later due to complexity and fragility.

---

## Data Model (Simplified)

### Postgres (Policies)
- `policies` (current editable state)
- `policy_versions` (immutable snapshot metadata + blob)
- `principal_overrides` (rate/burst/mode/expiry)

### Redis Keys
- Authoritative bucket (owner region only for this key):
  - `auth:{policy_id}:{principal_hash}:{descriptor_hash}`
- Local credits (any region):
  - `credit:{policy_id}:{principal_hash}:{descriptor_hash}`
- Dedupe (optional):
  - `dedupe:{request_id}:{key_hash}` with TTL 5–30s

Removed: global lease tables, CAS versions, multi-row invariants.

---

## APIs (Simplified)

### Decision API (HTTP or gRPC)
- `Check(request_id, principal, descriptors, policy_snapshot_version?) -> decision, retry_after_ms, headers`

### Owner Reserve API (Internal)
- `Reserve(key, desired_tokens) -> granted_tokens | deny + retry_after_ms`
- Only used by RLS-to-RLS; never by the gateway filter directly.

### Policy/Admin API
- `POST/PUT` policies, `POST :publish`, `POST :rollback`, overrides, emergency block.

---

## Removed Dependencies

- **Token Lease Coordinator**: replaced by per-key owner region (single-writer) and simple `Reserve`.
- **Lease State DB (Spanner/FDB/DynamoDB+CAS)**: removed entirely.
- **Kafka/PubSub + Policy Subscriber services**: replaced with snapshot polling from object storage.
- **Dedicated analytics store + decision log stream**: default to metrics + sampled logs in existing logging; add warehouse later only if needed.

---

## Merged Components

- **Coordinator + RLS** → RLS handles both:
  - local decisioning
  - owner-region authoritative consumes (`Reserve`)
- **Policy subscriber + caches** → built into Edge filter and RLS via snapshot polling.
- **Multiple databases** → Postgres for policies; Redis for runtime counters/credits.

---

## Justified Complexity (What Remains, and Why)

- **Edge L1 cache + single-flight**: required to keep latency low and protect RLS/Redis during bursts.
- **Redis scripting/atomicity**: required for correctness under concurrency at high QPS.
- **Per-key owner routing**: simplest way to get global correctness without a global coordinator/DB.
- **Snapshot-based policy consumption**: prevents request-path DB reads and enables safe rollback.
- **Bounded overshoot via short-lived credits**: unavoidable trade-off if you want low latency and regional isolation without per-request cross-region calls.

---

## Simplification Notes

- Removed: `Token Lease Coordinator` and `Lease DB` — acceptable because per-key owner region provides a single source of truth without global CAS bookkeeping.
- Removed: `Kafka/PubSub` policy distribution — acceptable because snapshot polling is resilient, simpler to operate, and fast enough for typical policy change rates.
- Removed: separate `Policy Subscriber + Snapshot Cache` service — acceptable because edge/RLS can poll and cache snapshots directly.
- Removed: dedicated analytics pipeline/store — acceptable because metrics + sampled logs cover SLOs/debugging initially; add warehousing only if requirements prove it.
- Merged: coordinator responsibilities into `Regional RLS` — acceptable because “owner routing” is a simple extension of decisioning logic and deploys everywhere.
- Complexity remaining: Redis atomic operations, L1 caching, and owner routing — necessary for correctness under concurrency, low latency, and global enforcement without per-request cross-region calls.
